WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.062
A C array is a contiguous sequence of elements of the same type.

2
00:00:04.062 --> 00:00:09.329
If an array has ten int elements, valid element indexes run from zero through nine.

3
00:00:09.329 --> 00:00:15.422
The array object has a fixed extent, and the code must keep reads and writes inside that extent.

4
00:00:15.422 --> 00:00:25.323
This simple rule becomes harder to preserve when arrays are passed through functions, converted to pointers, or paired with separately stored length values.

5
00:00:25.323 --> 00:00:28.179
Pointer arithmetic is scaled by element size.

6
00:00:28.179 --> 00:00:33.002
Advancing an int pointer by one moves to the next int, not to the next byte.

7
00:00:33.002 --> 00:00:37.572
Advancing a structure pointer by one moves by the size of the structure.

8
00:00:37.572 --> 00:00:45.949
This scaling is useful, but it also means reviewers must know which type the pointer has and what object it is supposed to traverse.

9
00:00:45.949 --> 00:00:49.820
Array names often decay to pointers when passed to functions.

10
00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:56.357
A function parameter written to look like an array parameter is usually treated as a pointer parameter.

11
00:00:56.357 --> 00:01:04.481
Once that decay occurs, the callee generally receives an address, not a complete array object with its original length attached.

12
00:01:04.481 --> 00:01:10.510
The original capacity must be passed separately or represented through a surrounding structure.

13
00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:14.509
A pointer by itself does not carry reliable bounds information.

14
00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:24.853
It may point to the first element of an array, the middle of an array, a single object, allocated storage, a string literal, or storage owned by another component.

15
00:01:24.853 --> 00:01:31.010
Without a valid length or contract, a callee cannot safely infer how many elements are available.

16
00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.817
The sizeof operator is a frequent source of review mistakes.

17
00:01:34.817 --> 00:01:41.101
Applied to an actual array in the same scope, sizeof can report the size of the whole array object.

18
00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:47.511
Applied to a pointer parameter, it reports the size of the pointer itself, not the pointed-to region.

19
00:01:47.511 --> 00:01:54.492
Code should not rely on a callee recovering an original array length with sizeof after array-to-pointer decay.

20
00:01:54.492 --> 00:01:57.221
Defensive APIs make this behavior explicit.

21
00:01:57.221 --> 00:02:02.996
If a function reads a buffer, pass the number of valid elements or bytes available to read.

22
00:02:02.996 --> 00:02:06.296
If a function writes, pass the destination capacity.

23
00:02:06.296 --> 00:02:11.627
If it appends, pass both current count and capacity or use a structured buffer type.

24
00:02:11.627 --> 00:02:14.483
The pointer is only one part of the contract.
